@meta
id: 28-14
title: Drill — one setlocal for markdown
chapter: 28
type: drill
skills: vimrc-filetype
par: 54
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: "[markdown]": { "editor.wordWrap": "on" }
open: after/ftplugin/markdown.vim

@brief
Eight times: add the wrap settings to the end of the markdown ftplugin
and save.

    setlocal textwidth=80
    setlocal linebreak

Nothing new. The drill is the file, not the keys — after eight goes,
"this is a markdown thing, so it goes in the markdown ftplugin" should
be a reflex rather than a decision.

@keys
| `G` `o` | open a line at the bottom |
| `setlocal` | this buffer only |

@hints
`G` then `o`, both lines in one insert session, Esc, `:wq`.
--
The last line in the file is a `setlocal`, not a comment, so Enter in
the middle of the insert does not add a `"`.

@solution
    G o setlocal textwidth=80<Enter> setlocal linebreak <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`textwidth=80` makes vim break the line as you type past column 80, so
`gq` and `gqap` have something to aim at. `linebreak` is about display
only: a line too long for the window wraps at a space instead of
mid-word. They are unrelated settings that happen to be wanted by the
same person on the same day.

Prose files are where per-filetype settings pay off most obviously,
because everything you want for prose — spell check, a text width, no
line numbers — is exactly what you do not want for code.

@start after/ftplugin/markdown.vim
" Markdown: prose, not code
setlocal spell
setlocal spelllang=en_gb

@expected after/ftplugin/markdown.vim
" Markdown: prose, not code
setlocal spell
setlocal spelllang=en_gb
setlocal textwidth=80
setlocal linebreak
